### What is a constructor and can it be private?

A constructor initializes an object when created with new. It has the same name as the class and no return type.

### What are the constructor rules?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // Constructor: same name as class, no return type
    public Account(String accountId, BigDecimal initialBalance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = initialBalance;
    }

    // Overloaded constructor
    public Account(String accountId) {
        this(accountId, BigDecimal.ZERO); // chains to first constructor
    }
}
// Rules:
// 1. Same name as class
// 2. No return type (not even void)
// 3. Called automatically by new
// 4. Can be overloaded
// 5. If you write none: Java provides default no-arg
// 6. If you write any: Java does not provide default
```

### How does the Singleton pattern use a private constructor?

```java
public class RbiConnectionManager {
    private static final RbiConnectionManager INSTANCE = new RbiConnectionManager();

    private RbiConnectionManager() {
        // Initialize connection pool, SSL certificates
        System.out.println("RBI connection initialized (happens once)");
    }

    public static RbiConnectionManager getInstance() {
        return INSTANCE;
    }

    public String sendReport(String report) {
        return "Sent to RBI: " + report;
    }
}
// RbiConnectionManager.getInstance() == RbiConnectionManager.getInstance() -> true
// new RbiConnectionManager() -> compile error: constructor is private

// Modern alternative: enum Singleton (thread-safe, serialization-safe)
public enum RbiConnection {
    INSTANCE;
    public String sendReport(String report) { return "Sent: " + report; }
}
```

### How does the Factory Method pattern use a private constructor?

```java
public class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    private final String type;
    private final BigDecimal balance;
    private final BigDecimal interestRate;

    private Account(String id, String type, BigDecimal balance, BigDecimal rate) {
        this.accountId = id;
        this.type = type;
        this.balance = balance;
        this.interestRate = rate;
    }

    public static Account createSavings(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        return new Account(id, "SAVINGS", balance, new BigDecimal("0.04"));
    }

    public static Account createCurrent(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        return new Account(id, "CURRENT", balance, BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
}
// Account.createSavings("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000")) is clear and self-documenting.
// new Account("SAV2001", "SAVINGS", new BigDecimal("125000"), new BigDecimal("0.04")) is unclear.
```

### How does the Utility Class pattern use a private constructor?

```java
public final class InterestCalculator {
    private InterestCalculator() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Utility class");
    }

    public static BigDecimal simpleInterest(BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal rate, int years) {
        return principal.multiply(rate).multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(years));
    }

    public static BigDecimal compoundInterest(BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal rate, int years) {
        return principal.multiply(BigDecimal.ONE.add(rate).pow(years)).subtract(principal);
    }
}
// InterestCalculator.simpleInterest(...) works.
// new InterestCalculator() -> compile error.
```

### How do the three patterns compare?

| Pattern | Purpose | Instance count | Example |
|---------|---------|---------------|----------|
| Singleton | One shared instance | Exactly 1 | RBI connection manager |
| Factory Method | Named creation | Many | Account.createSavings() |
| Utility Class | No instances | Zero | InterestCalculator |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting that writing any constructor removes the default no-arg constructor.

```java
// WRONG: parent has parameterized constructor, child expects default
public class Account {
    public Account(String id) { this.id = id; } // no default no-arg
}
public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    public SavingsAccount() {
        // Java inserts super() -> compile error: Account has no no-arg constructor
    }
}

// CORRECT: explicitly call super(args)
public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    public SavingsAccount() {
        super("DEFAULT"); // explicit call to parameterized constructor
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- For Singleton, use an enum (thread-safe, serialization-safe) or Spring @Component (singleton scope by default).
- For factory methods, records with static of() or from() methods are the modern approach.
- Utility classes should be marked final to prevent subclassing.
- Effective Java Item 1: consider static factory methods instead of constructors.
